    <Routine name="BorgRating"/>
    <LoopInitiator loopType="TrialHandler" name="trials_noise">
      <Param name="Selected rows" updates="None" val="" valType="str"/>
      <Param name="conditions" updates="None" val="[{'Noise': 'Babble', 'Level': 'High', 'Duration': 3}, {'Noise': 'Babble', 'Level': 'High', 'Duration': 3}, {'Noise': 'Babble', 'Level': 'High', 'Duration': 3}, {'Noise': 'Babble', 'Level': 'High', 'Duration': 3}, {'Noise': 'Babble', 'Level': 'High', 'Duration': 3}]" valType="str"/>
      <Param name="conditionsFile" updates="None" val="VLT_params.xlsx" valType="file"/>
      <Param name="endPoints" updates="None" val="[0, 1]" valType="num"/>
      <Param name="isTrials" updates="None" val="True" valType="bool"/>
      <Param name="loopType" updates="None" val="sequential" valType="str"/>
      <Param name="nReps" updates="None" val="1" valType="num"/>
      <Param name="name" updates="None" val="trials_noise" valType="code"/>
      <Param name="random seed" updates="None" val="" valType="code"/>
    </LoopInitiator>
    <Routine name="VLT_init"/>
    <LoopInitiator loopType="TrialHandler" name="VLT_stim">
      <Param name="Selected rows" updates="None" val="$pages" valType="str"/>
      <Param name="conditions" updates="None" val="[{'paragraph_text': 'Clouds are made of tiny water droplets or ice crystals that float high in the sky. They form when water from oceans, lakes, and rivers turns into water vapor. Water vapor is water in the form of gas, and it rises into the air. As it moves higher, the air gets colder. When the water vapor cools down enough, it sticks to small pieces of dust or pollen floating in the air. This process is called condensation. When many droplets group together, they form a cloud. There are different types of clouds. Some are thin and stretched out, while others are thick and puffy. The type of cloud depends on how high it is and how much water is inside it. Scientists who study weather look at clouds to help predict rain, snow, and storms. Clouds play an important role in moving water around our planet. '}, {'paragraph_text': 'Sea turtles are reptiles that live in the ocean. There are seven different species of sea turtles found around the world. They live in both warm and cool waters. Sea turtles spend most of their lives swimming, but females come onto sandy beaches to lay their eggs. They dig a deep hole in the sand, lay their eggs inside, and then cover them back up. After about two months, the baby turtles hatch and crawl across the beach toward the water. Sea turtles eat different things depending on what kind they are. Some eat jellyfish, while others eat seagrass or crabs. They must come up to the surface of the water on a regular basis to breathe air. Sea turtles are strong swimmers and can travel thousands of miles in their lifetime. Scientists study them to learn more about life in the ocean. '}, {'paragraph_text': 'Plants make their own food using a process called photosynthesis. To do this, they need three things: sunlight, water, and a gas called carbon dioxide. Carbon dioxide is found in the air all around us. Plants take in water through their roots, which are buried in the soil. They take in carbon dioxide through tiny holes on their leaves. When sunlight hits the leaves, the plant uses that energy to turn the water and carbon dioxide into sugar. This sugar is the food the plant uses to grow. During this process, the plant also makes oxygen, which is the gas that humans and animals need to breathe. That means plants help keep the air clean and healthy. Photosynthesis happens mostly in the leaves because that is where the plant catches the most sunlight. Without this process, life on Earth would look very different. '}, {'paragraph_text': 'Fossils are the remains or traces of plants and animals that lived a very long time ago. Most fossils form when a living thing dies and gets buried under layers of mud, sand, or dirt. Over many thousands of years, the layers harden into rock, and the shape of the plant or animal is preserved inside. Some fossils are bones or teeth, while others are prints left behind by leaves or footprints. Scientists who study fossils are called paleontologists. They dig carefully into rock to find fossils and learn what life was like long ago. Fossils have helped us learn about animals that no longer exist, like dinosaurs. They also show us how the Earth has changed over time. Some places that are dry deserts today were once covered by oceans. Fossils give us clues about the past that we could not get any other way. '}, {'paragraph_text': 'Water is always moving around our planet in a process called the water cycle. It starts when the sun heats up water in oceans, lakes, and rivers. The heat turns some of the water into water vapor, which is a gas that rises into the air. This step is called evaporation. As the water vapor rises higher, it cools down and turns back into tiny water droplets. This is called condensation, and it is how clouds are formed. When the droplets inside a cloud get heavy enough, they fall back down to the ground as rain, snow, sleet, or hail. This step is called precipitation. The water then flows into streams, rivers, and oceans, or it soaks into the ground. From there, the cycle starts all over again. The water cycle has been repeating for millions of years and is important for all life on Earth. '}, {'paragraph_text': 'Bridges are structures built to help people cross over water, roads, or valleys. People have been building bridges for thousands of years. The earliest bridges were simple logs or flat stones placed across a stream. Over time, people learned how to build stronger and longer bridges using wood, stone, and metal. Today, most large bridges are made of steel and concrete. There are several types of bridges. Beam Bridges are straight, flat bridges that are held up by supports on both ends. Arch bridges have a curved shape that helps spread out the weight. Suspension bridges hang from thick cables attached to tall towers. Engineers are the people who design bridges. They have to think about how much weight the bridge will hold, how long it needs to be, and what materials will work best. Building a bridge can take many months or even several years to complete. '}, {'paragraph_text': 'Migration is when animals travel long distances to find food, warmer weather, or a safe place to have their babies. Many types of animals migrate, including birds, fish, and mammals. Some birds fly thousands of miles south when the weather gets cold and return north when it warms up again. They follow the same paths year after year. Certain types of\xa0whales\xa0swim from\xa0cold waters\xa0to warm waters to give birth, then swim back again. Butterflies also migrate. One well-known type travels south every fall and returns north in the spring. Scientists are still learning how animals know where to go. Some use the position of the sun or stars to find their way. Others may use the Earth’s magnetic field, which acts like a built-in compass. Migration is a long and difficult journey, but it helps animals survive through changing seasons.\xa0'}, {'paragraph_text': 'Sound is made when something\xa0vibrates, or\xa0moves back and forth very quickly. When an object vibrates, it pushes the air around it. This creates waves that travel through the air and reach our ears. Our ears pick up these waves and send signals to our brain, which tells us what we are hearing. Sound can travel through more than just air. It also moves through water and solid objects like walls and floors. In fact, sound travels faster through water than through air, and even faster through solid materials. That is why you can sometimes hear sounds through a closed door. Loud sounds are made by strong vibrations, and quiet sounds come from weak vibrations. High-pitched sounds, like a whistle, come from fast vibrations. Low-pitched sounds, like\xa0a drum, come from slow vibrations. Sound cannot travel through empty space, which is why outer space is completely silent.\xa0'}, {'paragraph_text': 'There are three main types of rocks, and each one forms in\xa0a different way. The first type forms when hot melted rock cools down and hardens. This can happen above or below the surface of the Earth. The second type forms when small pieces of sand, mud, shells, or other materials build up in layers over a long time. The weight of the layers above presses down and turns the loose pieces into solid rock. The third\xa0type\xa0forms when existing rocks are changed by extreme heat or pressure deep underground. These forces can change the texture and appearance of the rock without melting it completely. Over millions of years, rocks can change from one type to another. This process is called the rock cycle. Rocks are found everywhere on Earth, from the tops of mountains to the bottom of the ocean floor, and scientists study them closely.\xa0'}, {'paragraph_text': 'Earth has four seasons: spring, summer, fall, and winter. The seasons change because the Earth is tilted on its axis, which is an imaginary line that runs through the planet from top to bottom. As the Earth moves around the sun, this tilt causes\xa0different parts\xa0of the planet to receive different amounts of sunlight throughout the year. When one half of the Earth is tilted toward the sun, that half gets more direct sunlight and experiences summer. At the same time, the other half is tilted away and experiences winter. In between, we have spring and\xa0fall, when\xa0the amount of sunlight is more balanced. The seasons affect the weather, how long each day lasts, and how plants and animals behave. Some trees lose their leaves in the fall, and many animals change their habits depending on the season. This pattern repeats every year as Earth continues its path around the sun.\xa0'}, {'paragraph_text': 'Insects are the largest group of animals on Earth. There are more types of insects than\xa0any\xa0other\xa0animal\xa0combined. Every insect has three main body parts: a head, a middle section, and a back section. They also have six legs, which is one way to tell them apart from spiders, which have eight. Many insects have wings and can fly, but not all of them do. Insects have a hard outer covering instead of bones inside their bodies. This outer shell protects them and helps keep their bodies from drying out. Insects use antennas on their heads to smell, touch, and sense the world around them. Some insects live alone, while others live in large groups. Ants and bees are examples of insects that live and work together in colonies. Insects can be found\xa0almost everywhere\xa0on Earth, from forests and deserts to cities and backyards.\xa0'}, {'paragraph_text': 'Maps are drawings that show what a place looks like from above. They help people find where things are and how to get from one place to another. Maps can show many things, including roads, rivers, mountains, and buildings. Some maps show an entire country, while others show just a small neighborhood. Most maps include a tool called a compass rose, which shows the directions north, south, east, and west. Maps also include a scale, which helps you figure out the real distance between two places. A legend, or key, explains what the symbols and colors on the map mean. People have been making maps for thousands of years. Long ago, maps were drawn by hand on paper. Today, many maps are made using computers and can be viewed on phones and tablets. Maps are useful for travelers, drivers, hikers, and anyone who needs\xa0directions.\xa0'}, {'paragraph_text': 'Taking care of your body helps you stay strong, feel good, and have energy throughout the day. One of the most important things you can do is eat a variety of healthy foods. Fruits, vegetables, grains, and proteins all give your body what it needs to grow and stay well. Drinking plenty of water is also important because your body uses water for\xa0almost everything\xa0it does. Exercise is another key part of staying healthy. Moving your body every day helps keep your heart, muscles, and bones strong. It does not have to be anything fancy. Walking, running, playing outside, or riding a bike all count.\xa0Sleeping\xa0is just as important as food and exercise. When you sleep, your body repairs itself and your brain sorts through everything you learned that day. Most kids need about nine to twelve hours of sleep each night to feel their best.\xa0'}, {'paragraph_text': 'When winter comes and temperatures drop, animals have\xa0different ways\xa0of staying warm. Some animals grow thicker fur or feathers as the cold season approaches. This extra layer traps heat close to their bodies and keeps the\xa0cold air\xa0out. Other animals survive winter by hibernating. During hibernation, an animal goes into a deep sleep. Its heart beats much slower, and its body temperature drops. This helps the animal save energy when food is hard to find. Some animals store extra fat in their bodies before winter begins. This fat gives them energy while they rest. Other animals, like certain birds, avoid the cold entirely by flying to warmer places until spring returns. Some smaller animals, like mice and rabbits, stay active all winter but spend more time in underground burrows or nests where it is warmer. Each of these methods helps animals survive until the weather warms up again.\xa0'}, {'paragraph_text': 'Soil is much more than just dirt. It is a mixture of tiny pieces of rock, dead plants, water, air, and living things.\xa0Over many years, wind, rain, and ice\xa0have broken\xa0large rocks into smaller and smaller pieces.\xa0These tiny pieces of rock mix with dead leaves, roots, and other plant material that has broken down over time. This mixture forms the soil we see on the ground. Soil also\xa0contains\xa0small living things like worms, insects, and bacteria. These creatures help break down dead plants and animals even further, which adds nutrients to the soil. Plants need these nutrients to grow.\xa0Different places\xa0have\xa0different types\xa0of soil.\xa0Some soil is sandy and dry, while other soil is dark and rich with nutrients.\xa0Farmers pay close attention to their soil because healthy soil helps crops grow well. Without soil, most plants on Earth would not be able to survive.\xa0'}, {'paragraph_text': 'The moon is the closest object in space to our planet. It does not make its own light. Instead, it reflects light from the sun, which is what makes it look bright\xa0in\xa0the night sky. As the moon moves around the Earth, we see different amounts of\xa0moon\xa0that are lit up.\xa0These different shapes are called phases. Sometimes we see the full round shape, which is called a full moon. Other times we see only a thin sliver, which is called a crescent. There are times when we cannot see the moon at all because the lit side is facing away from us.\xa0It takes about one month for the moon to go through all of its phases and start over again.\xa0People have watched the moon’s phases for thousands of years. Farmers once used the phases to decide when to plant and harvest their crops. The moon continues to be studied by scientists today.\xa0'}, {'paragraph_text': 'Rivers are powerful forces that change the land around them over time. As water flows downhill, it picks up small pieces of rock, sand, and soil and carries them along. This process is called erosion. Over many years, the moving water can carve deep valleys and widen\xa0canyons into the earth. Rivers do not always follow the same path. They can shift and bend over time, creating curved shapes across flat land. When a river slows down, it drops the materials it has been carrying. This is called\xa0deposition. The dropped sand and soil can build up and form new land along the riverbank or at the river’s mouth where it meets the ocean. Rivers also provide water for plants, animals, and people living nearby. Many towns and cities were built near rivers because the water was useful for drinking, farming, and moving goods from place to place.\xa0'}, {'paragraph_text': 'Have you ever touched a doorknob and felt a small shock? That shock is caused by static electricity. Everything around us is made of tiny particles that are too small to see. Some of these particles carry a charge, which can be positive or negative. Most of the time, objects have an equal number of positive and negative\xa0charges,\xa0and everything is balanced. But when two objects rub together, some charges can move from one object to the other. This creates an imbalance. One object ends up with extra negative charges, and the other\xa0does not have enough.\xa0When the object with extra charges touches something else, the charges jump to balance out again. That jump is the shock you feel. This is why your hair might stand up after you pull off a winter hat, or why a balloon can stick to a wall after you rub it on your shirt. Static electricity is all around us.\xa0'}, {'paragraph_text': 'People have been measuring time for thousands of years. Long ago, people used the sun to tell\xa0the time. They noticed that shadows changed length and direction throughout the day. Some early tools used a stick or a post placed in the ground to track the\xa0shadow’s\xa0movement. Later,\xa0people built\xa0devices that used dripping water or flowing sand to measure short amounts of time. Candles marked with lines were also used, since each section would burn at a steady rate. Over time, people invented clocks with gears and springs that could keep time more accurately. Today, most clocks and watches are digital and use electronic signals to keep track of every second. We divide each day into twenty-four hours, each hour into sixty minutes, and each minute into sixty seconds. Measuring time helps us plan our days, arrive on time, and understand how long events last. It is a part of daily life.\xa0'}, {'paragraph_text': 'Light is what allows us to see the world around us.\xa0What we call white light is actually made up of many different colors mixed together.\xa0You can sometimes see these colors when light passes through raindrops and creates a rainbow. The colors include red, orange, yellow, green, blue, and violet. When light hits an object, the object absorbs some colors and reflects others. The color that bounces back to your eyes is the color you see. For example, a red apple reflects red light and absorbs\xa0the other\xa0colors. A green leaf reflects green light.\xa0Objects that look white are reflecting almost all of the light, while objects that look black are absorbing almost all of it.\xa0Inside your eyes, there are special cells that detect\xa0different colors\xa0and send signals to your brain. Your brain then puts the information together so you can see the colorful world around you.\xa0'}, {'paragraph_text': 'A pond is a small body of still water that is home to many kinds of living things. Plants grow along the edges and on the surface of the water. Some of these plants have roots in the mud at the bottom, while others float freely on top. These plants provide food and shelter for the animals that live in the pond. Fish, frogs, turtles, and insects are some of the most common pond animals. Frogs often lay their eggs in the water, and their young, called tadpoles, live in the pond until they grow\xa0their legs\xa0and can move onto land. Insects like dragonflies spend part of their lives in the water before becoming adults that\xa0fly. Birds and other animals that live nearby often visit ponds to drink or find food. Even a small pond can support a surprising number of plants and animals living together.\xa0'}]" valType="str"/>
      <Param name="conditionsFile" updates="None" val="Lectures_all.xlsx" valType="file"/>
      <Param name="endPoints" updates="None" val="[0, 1]" valType="num"/>
      <Param name="isTrials" updates="None" val="True" valType="bool"/>
      <Param name="loopType" updates="None" val="sequential" valType="str"/>
      <Param name="nReps" updates="None" val="99" valType="num"/>
      <Param name="name" updates="None" val="VLT_stim" valType="code"/>
      <Param name="random seed" updates="None" val="" valType="code"/>
    </LoopInitiator>
    <Routine name="STIMULI"/>
    <LoopTerminator name="VLT_stim"/>
    <Routine name="BorgRating"/>
    <LoopTerminator name="trials_noise"/>
    <LoopInitiator loopType="TrialHandler" name="trials_TLX">
      <Param name="Selected rows" updates="None" val="" valType="str"/>
      <Param name="conditions" updates="None" val="[{'TLX_Title': 'Performance', 'TLX_Description': 'How successful do you think you were in accomplishing the goals of the task set by the experimenter (or yourself)? How satisfied were you with your performance in accomplishing these goals?', 'TLX_Label_Num': 2}, {'TLX_Title': 'Effort', 'TLX_Description': 'How hard did you have to work (mentally and physically) to accomplish your level of performance?', 'TLX_Label_Num': 1}, {'TLX_Title': 'Frustration Level', 'TLX_Description': 'How insecure, discouraged, irritated, stressed and annoyed versus secure, gratified, content, relaxed and complacent did you feel during the task?', 'TLX_Label_Num': 1}]" valType="str"/>
      <Param name="conditionsFile" updates="None" val="TLX_short.xlsx" valType="file"/>
      <Param name="endPoints" updates="None" val="[0, 1]" valType="num"/>
      <Param name="isTrials" updates="None" val="True" valType="bool"/>
      <Param name="loopType" updates="None" val="sequential" valType="str"/>
      <Param name="nReps" updates="None" val="1" valType="num"/>
      <Param name="name" updates="None" val="trials_TLX" valType="code"/>
      <Param name="random seed" updates="None" val="" valType="code"/>
    </LoopInitiator>
    <Routine name="TLX"/>
    <LoopTerminator name="trials_TLX"/>
    <Routine name="Read"/>
    <Routine name="BorgRating"/>
    <Routine name="End"/>
  </Flow>
</PsychoPy2experiment>
